diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index ff92be649ff3f610b38798b3310751534d032814..79df835e301177417a2d18c1c816b25b7bf73670 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -3,3 +3,50 @@
 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## Contents
+
+### JSON Pointer (RFC 6901)
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` returns the value a JSON Pointer refers to:
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { items: [{ name: 'first' }], 'a/b': true };
+
+resolvePointer(document, '/items/0/name'); // 'first'
+resolvePointer(document, '/a~1b'); // true
+resolvePointer(document, ''); // the whole document
+```
+
+It throws a `JsonPointerError` when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer (`error.code` is
+`'invalid-pointer'`), or when the document has no value there (`'unresolved'`). The error message names
+the pointer and where the walk stopped. Notable points of the specification:
+
+- The empty pointer refers to the whole document; `'/'` refers to the member named `''`.
+- Inside a reference token, `~0` stands for `~` and `~1` for `/`. A `~` followed by anything else is a
+  syntax error, and `~1` is decoded before `~0`, so `~01` is the member named `~1`.
+- Array indices are decimal, without a sign and without leading zeros: `/0` resolves, `/00`, `/+0`,
+  `/-1` and `/1.0` do not.
+- The token `-`, which JSON Patch uses for the position after the last element, never resolves to a
+  value.
+- Only a document's own members are reachable: `/toString` and `/constructor` do not resolve unless the
+  document really has such a member.
+- The URI fragment notation of section 6 (`#/items/0`) is a different encoding and is not accepted;
+  strip the `#` and percent-decode it first.
+
+`JsonValue` models the JSON data model (`JSON.parse` output). Because `undefined` is not a JSON value,
+a type with optional properties is not a `JsonValue`; cast such values at the call site.
+
+## Development
+
+Node's own TypeScript support runs the sources directly, so the tests need no build step. Within the
+library, modules import each other with explicit `.ts` extensions; `tsc` rewrites them when it emits
+`dist`.
+
+```sh
+npm test        # node --test
+npm run typecheck
+npm run build
+```
